Myth: In the age of unchanging twilight, the world lay suspended between existence and emptiness, where nothing grew and nothing died.

In a silent valley lived an old gardener named Mira, who tended a single withered tree. One evening, she discovered a wounded phoenix struggling in the dust, its flames nearly extinguished. Though she had little warmth to spare, she wrapped it in her only blanket and shared her meager fire.

As the phoenix recovered, it sang a haunting melody of cycles—birth and death, growth and decay, joy and sorrow. Mira understood that the world's stillness came from lacking these rhythms.

